+[[profiling-dfs]]
+===== Profiling DFS
+
+The dfs phase collects term statistics to achieve more accurate search results
+prior to the query phase. To profile the dfs phase use a `_search` request with
+the url parameter `search_type` set as `dfs_and_then_query` and add a top-level
+`profile` parameter set to `true`. Note that for the dfs phase to run an index
+must have multiple shards.
+
+As an example, let's first setup an index with multiple shards and index
+a pair of documents with different values on a keyword field.
+
+[source,console]
+--------------------------------------------------
+PUT my-dfs-index
+{
+  "settings": {
+    "number_of_shards": 2, <1>
+    "number_of_replicas": 1
+  },
+  "mappings": {
+      "properties": {
+        "my-keyword": { "type": "keyword" }
+      }
+    }
+}
+
+POST my-dfs-index/_bulk?refresh=true
+{ "index" : { "_id" : "1" } }
+{ "my-keyword" : "a" }
+{ "index" : { "_id" : "2" } }
+{ "my-keyword" : "b" }
+--------------------------------------------------
+<1> The `my-dfs-index` is created with multiple shards.
+
+With an index setup, we can now profile the dfs phase of a
+search query. As an example we use a term query.
+
+[source,console]
+--------------------------------------------------
+GET /my-dfs-index/_search?search_type=dfs_query_then_fetch&pretty <1>
+{
+  "profile": true, <2>
+  "query": {
+    "term": {
+      "my-keyword": {
+        "value": "a"
+      }
+    }
+  }
+}
+--------------------------------------------------
+// TEST[continued]
+<1> The `search_type` url parameter is set to `dfs_query_then_fetch` to
+ensure the dfs phase is run.
+<2> The `profile` parameter is set to `true`.
+
+In the response, we see a very verbose profile which includes a `dfs` section
+for each shard along with profile output for the rest of the search phases.
+One of the `dfs` sections for a shard looks like the following:
+
+[source,js]
+--------------------------------------------------
+{
+    ...
+    "dfs" : {
+        "statistics" : {
+            "type" : "statistics",
+            "description" : "collect term statistics",
+            "time_in_nanos" : 236955,
+            "breakdown" : {
